1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,890
Imagine spending millions designing, uh, the perfect digital roadmap for your ministry

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:11,600
only to watch every single department just ignore it the second a deadline looms.

3
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:12,780
Yeah, it happens all the time.

4
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:20,800
Right. So today's deep dive is based on a stack of case studies and architectural guidelines covering phase five of the progressive framework.

5
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:26,440
And our mission here is to show you how to give a national enterprise architecture actual teeth.

6
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:32,299
Exactly. Stopping your ministry systems from being siloed and, you know, costly to change.

7
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:37,970
So we're looking specifically at the execute and govern phase, which is the stage that essentially never ends.

8
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:38,940
Right. It's ongoing.

9
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:48,700
Exactly. It's the mechanics of taking an enterprise architecture, which is, uh, often just a theoretical pipeline, and turning it into a living practice that people actually follow.

10
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,550
Because to execute an architecture across an entire government,

11
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:54,060
you first need a single source of truth.

12
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:57,120
Right. Otherwise, everyone is working off different playbooks.

13
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:01,340
But how do we prevent that approved roadmap from just gathering dust?

14
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:11,660
Like- Mm-hmm. -is this essentially like setting up a permanent constitutional committee where a dedicated group is always interpreting the rules rather than relying on a temporary task force?

15
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:16,240
Well, that constitutional committee metaphor works really well because it requires permanence.

16
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,179
The foundation here is a live repository.

17
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,670
Okay, so not just a shared folder on a network drive.

18
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:26,380
No, no, not at all. It's the definitive store for progressive architecture.

19
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,260
It actively holds the technical layers, the sourcing decisions, meaning, you know, who supplies what.

20
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:34,220
Right. Plus the core principles and a running decision log.

21
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:38,000
So there's a living record of not just what was decided, but why.

22
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,770
Exactly. And that requires actual humans to manage it.

23
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:46,920
So there is a permanent team of two to four architects reporting directly to the digitalization officer.

24
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:52,580
Oh, okay. Yeah. With one named architect keeping the repository current as each project wave delivers.

25
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:56,180
This team is the standing muscle for cross-cutting decisions.

26
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,600
And they exist whether a specific project is running or not.

27
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,200
Right. They are always there.

28
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:06,760
Well, having that standing muscle is crucial, but let's test it against a real world scenario from the case studies.

29
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,820
Sure. Say a new scholarship program arrives.

30
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,560
They're heavily funded, and their deadline is tight.

31
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:15,140
They plan to build their own small learner list.

32
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:21,670
Because integrating with the government's authoritative learner register means waiting in line for that team's availability.

33
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:26,549
Exactly. And honestly, if I'm running that scholarship project and the clock is ticking,

34
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:31,660
going rogue and building my own list is the only logical choice to hit my deadline.

35
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,800
Well, in a vacuum, sure. I mean, it solves your immediate problem.

36
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:43,060
Right. But for the government as a whole, it means the exact same citizen data is duplicated, rekeyed, and fragmented across different systems.

37
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:43,440
Oh, I see.

38
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:46,280
Yeah. And this is why the review gate exists.

39
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:54,120
The gate intercepts the project and asks a definitive question like, "Does a shared building block already exist for this function?"

40
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:57,220
And for this scholarship program, the answer is yes.

41
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:01,620
Yes, because the learner register was established as authoritative back in wave one.

42
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:07,840
So if the review gate let them proceed, the government would end up with a fourth separate learner list.

43
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:13,640
Exactly. The data becomes highly fragmented, locked into one supplier, and completely out of step.

44
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:18,780
But, you know, individual projects will always try to bypass shared systems for speed.

45
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:20,049
It's just human nature.

46
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:21,299
A hundred percent.

47
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:29,080
So who actually has the authority to stop a fully funded project in its tracks and enforce the enterprise architecture?

48
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,680
Well, that authority sits with the board.

49
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:37,780
So in this scenario, they mandate that the project must consume the existing learner register.

50
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,790
Wow. Okay. Yeah. They catch the fragmentation while it's still just a line in a project plan long before any code is written.

51
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:48,860
Because standard procurement rules can't achieve this level of intervention, right?

52
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:52,520
No, they really can't. I mean, you can't buy your way out of duplicated systems.

53
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:56,400
It requires a whole of government view and a board that's willing to just say no.

54
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,540
And I imagine that no doesn't just vanish into the ether.

55
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,300
It goes straight into that live repository's decision log.

56
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:12,560
Right. It gets recorded with the full rationale, and then four times a year, the board brings the business side and the IT side together over that exact same repository.

57
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:13,560
Look at the same data.

58
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:17,560
Exactly. And they make these binding decisions together forever.

59
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:21,579
So phase five works because it relies on three interconnected elements:

60
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:28,380
a live repository that maintains the truth, a review gate that catches duplicated systems before they're built,

61
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,160
and a board with the teeth to enforce alignment.

62
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:34,900
Right. It forces collaboration where fragmentation naturally occurs.

63
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:40,140
It transforms the architecture from a dusty blueprint into an active governing mechanism.

